abstract: >-
  Dobrushin's uniqueness criterion in its Wasserstein (W1) form, applied to the SU(2) Wilson lattice gauge measure on Z^4. With the Euclidean
  (chordal) metric of R^4 on SU(2) = S^3 (Levin's metric), the one-site coefficient in a field of strength kappa equals
  E_kappa[x_perp^2] = I_2(kappa)/(kappa I_1(kappa)) for every kappa >= 0 (v1.4: kappa <= 4/3), strictly decreasing in kappa, maximal at zero field where it equals 1/4;
  each of the 18 neighbouring links has influence exactly beta/4, the Dobrushin sum is 9 beta/2, and the Gibbs measure is unique for beta < 2/9
  (weight exp(-beta sum_p (1 - Re Tr U_p / 2))). The proof of the identity is analytic and not formalised in Lean; the v1.4 interval-arithmetic proof
  on kappa <= 4/3, with Lean-checked final rational steps, is kept as a second proof. The bound 1/4 for all fields, the exactness of beta/4 and the
  range beta < 2/9 were proved before by Bhattacharjee and Bhattacharya (Hexagon 2610.00341, Theorems E, F, 7, Proposition 13 of v1); this note makes
  no priority claim on them. With the geodesic metric the exact coefficient is 8 beta/(9 pi) (kappa <= 3 pi/8), giving the weaker range beta < pi/16.
  A remark records the Poincare inequality for beta < 2/9 that Wu's theorem (2006) gives. As an application of the chordal coefficient, Dobrushin's comparison for
  the slab sigma-models of Durhuus and Froehlich gives Wilson's area law for beta < 2/3, uniformly on tori, with free boundary conditions and for every Gibbs measure,
  with string tension at least arcosh(2/beta - 2); this range is not new (Borgs and Seiler 1983, Appendix B of the preprint version, confinement for beta < 12/17), and no
  priority is claimed for any threshold. Apart from this application, no decay of correlations and no log-Sobolev inequality is claimed.
